    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d156">

                <pb facs="0254" n="148" sameAs="frus:frus1891/pg148"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Egan to Mr.
                            Blaine</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1891-07-28"><hi rendition="#italic">July 28, 1891</hi>.</date>
                        (Received September 26.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 183.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to my telegram of
                    March 8, and my dispatch No. 148, of April 1 last, conveying the request of the
                    Chilean Government that the United States would permit one of its war ships to
                    convey from Valparaiso to Montevideo a quantity of bar silver which it desired
                    to export for the purpose of paying the interest on the national debt abroad, to
                    which communication I did not receive any reply. I now beg to state that the
                    English Government has placed the British war ship <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Espiègle</hi> at the service of the Government of Chile to convey this bar
                    silver to England and that she is now taking it on board at Talcahuano.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Patrick Egan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
